okay so luke skywalker showing up at the  end of season two of the mandalorian was  completely mind-blowing for me it's the  best scene in a star wars property since  the original trilogy and there's a  number of reasons why it works so well  up until this point disney's handling of  star wars had been mixed to say the  least and the last jedi has had more  back and forth over it than whether hand  shot first or not the sequel trilogy was  a bit more clunky and the constant  changing of direction left a lot of the  most beloved characters feeling that  they didn't get the send-off that they  deserved luke skywalker was definitely  one of these and to see him tossing  lightsabers away drinking blue milk and  looking like nick nolte made many fans  feel like our last memories of the hero  wouldn't be pleasant ones now i actually  did quite enjoy the force projection  that we saw in the finale of the last  jedi but even then i did feel a bit  short-changed as it's not really him  going out in a blaze of glory like  obi-wan and anakin the rise of skywalker  did bring him back through the use of  force ghosts but for many it seemed like  the last memory of luke would be him  going out on his back as a shell of his  former self that is until the  mandalorian came along and brought the  character back in a big way that felt  like not only redemption for him but  also the franchise itself now up top  i'll say that i absolutely love both  seasons of the show i think the first  one did a really good job of recapturing  the feel of the star wars universe and  it did so without relying too heavily on  fan service we thought that we were  getting a holy original story and thus  when season 2 came along it made the  inclusion of the expanded lore  characters seem even more overwhelming  there was of course kobanth boba fett  ahsoka tano and lastly luke skywalker  who showed up in the final episode to  take roku with him now throughout the  season we watched as mando attempted to  locate the jedi in order for groku to  begin his training at the time there was  tons of theories flying around that he'd  be hooking up with kanan jairus ezra  bridger or any number of jedi that were  left out in the galaxy there's even some  that said mace windu i'm not joking go  back and watch the tapes now very few  people believe that it was luke and  whenever the fan theory got suggested to  me i quickly shut it down because i  truly thought that one it would cost way  too much to bring mark hamill back and  the age him two that he was done with a  franchise and three that it would have  leaked disney did an outstanding job of  keeping it a secret and as luke's x-wing  slowly rolled into the frame i have to  admit that i couldn't have been happier  to be wrong  at this point in the show the heroes  were at their lowest points and  completely surrounded by mafkidian's  forces it seemed like all hope had been  lost however hope is a theme that is  laced throughout the star wars saga and  seeing luke's x-wing flying to the  hangar made the hairs on the back of my  neck stand up on end i remember it like  it was yesterday and re-watching the  clip brought everything flooding back  now what the creative team did  brilliantly is that they hid luke's face  and still kept us sitting up throughout  the entire scene desperate to see the  reveal even though the black cloak gave  it away showing his arrival through  security cameras was a super smart way  to keep us glued to the scene it allowed  the moment to build up and hearing  motifs from luke's theme laced  throughout helped to create an  anticipation for what would be one of  the biggest star wars moments ever  finally we gotta look up close and  personal and the black cloaked figure  cut through all of the dark troopers  with ease as he moved towards the bridge  you have to remember that the dark  troopers were by far the most fearsome  foes in the mandalorian at this point  and seeing these terminator-esque  enemies getting cut down like they were  battle droids showed how powerful that  luke was now on a meta level luke's  arrival and the way that he travels  through the corridors is purposely shot  to resemble his father's attack in rogue  one one of the best darth vader scenes  of all time had him cutting up the rebel  forces and it's easily the high point of  that movie that feels like the perfect  ending for the film playing those scenes  side by side we see how luke and his dad  share a lot of things in common as  several of their moves mirror one  another it's sort of like poetry they  rhyme and just in the same way that his  father crushed someone using his hand  luke does the same thing with a machine  now just in the same way that the scene  brings us the action it also brings us  the satisfaction of balling like a baby  everyone took the mick out of star wars  theory for crying when luke showed up  but don't lie you did it too didn't you  search your feelings you know it to be  true now luke brought with him not only  a thrilling fight but watching mando  hand over his surrogate son two-packed a  punch that's really hard to forget over  the two seasons the pair had bonded and  though mano didn't want to give him away  he knew that he'd have a better life  with the jedi than he could provide  well as long as he doesn't attend school  at the same time as kylo but their  goodbye is a heartbreaking one and the  mandalorian creed that din jaron was a  part of were instructed not to remove  their helmets under any circumstances  it's something that din wrestled with  throughout both seasons and it was only  when it was absolutely necessary that he  decided to take it off however here he  broke the rules and removed it to say  farewell to grogu face to face for what  may be the final time though he said  he'd see him again we don't know if  that's gonna be true or not and if this  was to be the last ever mandalorian  season it's the perfect way to say  goodbye seeing the little guy reach out  and touch his cheek for the first time  was a heartbreaking moment and his dear  old dad din told him not to be afraid  though the pair didn't want to go they  knew they had to and r2d2 roamed in to  give groggoo someone else to bond with  luke picked up grogu and placed him on  his shoulder as he walked down the  hallway and this too carries a lot of  meaning with it this over the shoulder  shot is something that we've seen echo  throughout both seasons namely when din  was carrying groggu over his and it  perfectly mirrored those moments to show  that he had a new guardian luke's  spotting words were may the force be  with you and not only was this the  character saying it in but it was also  him very much saying it to the audience  who had been desperate to see this side  of luke once more  though the aging was imperfect and there  were a lot of flaws in it as a viewer  it's easy to look past these parts  because of the magic within the scene  that brings back exactly why we all fell  in love with star wars this is a  franchise that's had its ups and downs  over the years but scenes like this  served as a reminder of why it's still  so popular and why people stick with it  throughout their entire lives sure star  wars will have bad movies bad characters  and head scratching twists and turns  sometimes but it's moments like this  that we as a fan base hold out for and  it makes all the disappointments worth  it just to get these glimmers of hope  this was a perfect moment and not only  was it a joyful one but it was also  bittersweet in many regards we know that  mando has just lost the one person that  turned him from a hired gun into an  actual human but beyond that it leads to  an uncertain future as we touched upon  elia luke's jedi academy has somewhat of  a tragic history and just in the same  way that grogu survived order 66 he's  likely going to have to deal with the  rise of kylo ren there's an incredible  mix of emotions that come from this but  even with all that it's difficult not to  get caught up in this moment the music  is perfect the build-up to the scene is  perfect the way that luke mirrors his  father's actions is absolutely  incredible and at the heart of it is an  emotional farewell between a father and  son it really is one of the best moments  that star wars has to offer and it's a  scene that i absolutely love  now obviously i'd love to hear your  thoughts on it so make sure you comment  below and let me know if you agree with  us then please hit the thumbs up button  and don't forget to subscribe as we do  videos like this each and every week and  we'd love to have you around in case you  don't know i'm your host paul aka  lukewarm skywalker and we will be  covering star wars visions next week so  i'd love to see you then now we are in a  competition and giving away three copies  of zack snyder's dc trilogy on the 30th  of september and all you have to do to  be on the chance of winning is drop a  comment below with your thoughts on the  scene we picked the comments at random  at the end of the month and the winners  of the last one are on screen right now  so that's you then message me on twitter  heavyspoilers if you want something else  to watch then make sure you check out  our breakdown of what if episode 6 which  will be linked on screen right now we've  gone over the entire thing from top to  bottom and it's definitely worth  checking out so i'll see you over there  right now you better if not then thank  you for sending through this video i've  been paul you take care of yourself and  i'll see you on the next one peace
 

